# Troubleshooting protocol

When you hit an error, library issue, or unfamiliar behavior:

## Tier 1 — Check templates FIRST

Look in the relevant template file's "GOTCHAS THIS FILE ENCODES" docstring:
Haver: @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/data/haver_pull.py
Bloomberg: @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/data/bbg_xbbg.py
Macrobond: @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/data/macrobond_pull.py
FRED: @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/data/fred_pull.py
X-13 SA: @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/sa/x13_seasonal_adjust.py
Charts: @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/charts/renmac_chart_style.py
Email (MS Graph): @C:/Users/asingh/new_work/econ-templates/scripts/send_via_graph.py

If the issue is documented there, apply the documented fix. Do NOT
re-derive it from scratch and do NOT web_search if it's already in the
templates.

## Tier 2 — Web search official sources

If the issue is NOT in the templates, web_search and web_fetch:

- The library's official GitHub repo (issues, README, docs)
- statsmodels GitHub issues for statsmodels errors
- The xbbg, blpapi, fredapi GitHub repos for those tools
- Macrobond's developer documentation
- Stack Overflow only if the above don't have it

Prefer official sources over blog posts. For Python libraries, the
GitHub Issues tab almost always has the answer for non-trivial errors.

## Tier 3 — Update templates after resolving

After you fix a new bug, BEFORE moving on:

1. Print: "NEW GOTCHA — propose adding to <template_file>:"
2. Show the user (a) the error symptom, (b) the root cause, (c) the
   working fix, in the same format as existing gotchas in that file.
3. Wait for the user to say "add it" before editing the template.
4. If approved, append the gotcha to the docstring header AND fix
   the code beneath if needed.

Templates are the cumulative memory. Every new gotcha that lives only
in a project script is lost to future projects.

## What NOT to do

- Don't `pip install --upgrade` to "try fixing" a library issue. The
  shared venv versions are pinned for a reason. Surface the bug instead.
- Don't paper over an error with try/except that returns NaN silently.
  If a data pull fails, raise — the user needs to know.
- Don't web_search for things in the templates. That wastes a search
  call and signals you didn't read the template gotchas.
